 * texasinsects.tamu.edu/termite, citybugs ENT-2002, urbanentomology.tamu.edu,
 * texasinsects.tamu.edu/desert-termite and mytexas.ag.tamu.edu.
 *
 * SPECIES DISCIPLINE:
 *  - Write about Reticulitermes / native subterranean termites generically, or
 *    R. flavipes. E-368: "The major termite populations are in the coastal
 *    areas of Texas, but they are found throughout the state."
 *  - DO NOT claim Formosan termites. Coptotermes formosanus is Houston-
 *    Galveston and Beaumont-Port Arthur only.
 *  - R. virginicus stops at Uvalde/Tom Green; R. hageni at Bexar/Eastland.
 *    Both stop east of here.
 *  ⚠️ NOT VERIFIED — do not write: R. tibialis (arid-land subterranean) in
 *    Texas. Documented for AZ and CO; no Texas extension source found.
 *
 * ⚠️ NOT FOUND — do not write: any AgriLife publication quantifying termite
 *   pressure for the High Plains / Permian Basin, or saying the region is
 *   lower-risk than east Texas. The gradient statement above is all we have.
 *
 * 🚩 POPULAR-BELIEF CONFLICT, the core of this page: "it's too dry for termites
 *   out here" is unsupported by any extension source. E-368 is explicit that if
 *   moisture is available termites can be away from soil for extended periods,
 *   and names defective plumbing, leaky roofs, AC condenser leaks and poorly
 *   maintained gutters. Colorado State Extension is the strongest analogue and
 *   says termite problems there INCREASED because of landscape irrigation and
 *   poor drainage, with the belief that "there are no termites in Colorado"
 *   itself driving under-protection. Flagged on the page AS an analogue.
 *
 * 🚩 THE REGIONAL DIFFERENTIATOR: DESERT TERMITE, Gnathamitermes tubiformans.
 *   "Common in south and west Texas", conspicuous May-September on shortgrass
 *   pasture, builds fragile mud tubes and sheets around herbaceous and woody
 *   plants, litter, dung and fence posts. Workers 3/8 in, creamy white with
 *   brown heads. DOES NOT tunnel into wood, no structural damage, "medically
 *   harmless to man and animals". A rangeland issue, worse on overgrazed or
 *   drought-stressed ground.
 *
 * Swarm window for these counties: APRIL-MAY (Panhandle timing per E-368 and
 *   urbanentomology). South Texas is Jan-Feb. Flights in daylight, often after
 *   rain. Do not print the statewide Feb-May window as if it were local.
 *
 * Economics: ~$500M/yr Texas damage, ~70% chance of damage in 25 years for an
 *   unprotected home — STATE-LEVEL figures, and the page labels them as such.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Pest library', path: '/pest-library/' },
  { name: 'Subterranean termites', path: '/pest-library/subterranean-termite/' },
];

const quickAnswer =
