    a: 'Usually the first sign is swarmers themselves, or discarded wings on windowsills, in spider webs, or near indoor lights. Then mud tubes on piers, foundation walls, slabs and near utility openings — built from soil, wood particles and debris held together by salivary and faecal secretions. AgriLife is specific that the presence of shelter tubes and mud within galleries indicates an active infestation. Damage follows the wood grain, attacking the soft spring growth, and galleries are lined with mud or show a pale spotted appearance from soft faecal material.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the actual construction standards for a crawl space?',
    a: 'AgriLife gives figures. A minimum eighteen inches of clearance between floor joists and soil, and at least twelve inches between floor beams and soil. Cover at least seventy percent of the crawl space soil with six-mil polyethylene. Cross-ventilate at one square foot of vent per one hundred and fifty square feet of crawl space, with vents opposite each other and near the corners.',
  },
