---

<BaseLayout
  title="Subterranean Termites | West Texas Pest Library"
  description="Why the dry-climate assumption is wrong, when they swarm here, and how to tell a desert termite from a structural threat. Sourced to Texas A&amp;M AgriLife."
  path="/pest-library/subterranean-termite/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pest library · <em>Reticulitermes</em> species</p>
      <h1>Subterranean termites</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          There is a belief out here that this country is too dry for termites. It is the single most
          expensive misconception in West Texas property ownership, and it is not supported by any
          extension source we can find.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also self-reinforcing in an unusually damaging way. People who believe there are no
          termites do not have inspections, do not correct conducive conditions, and do not look at the
          foundation line — so activity runs undetected for years, and by the time anybody notices, the
          finding confirms nothing except that nobody was looking.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What the extension sources actually say</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>On distribution.</strong> AgriLife's E-368 is a gradient statement rather than a
          boundary: "The major termite populations are in the coastal areas of Texas, but they are found
          throughout the state." Fewer than the Gulf coast. Not none.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On the dryness argument, directly.</strong> E-368 notes that termites become dehydrated
          easily and need soil contact <em>or another moisture source</em> — and then names them: defective
          plumbing, leaky roofs, leaks from air conditioning condensers, poorly maintained gutters. It goes
          further and corrects an older belief explicitly: <strong>"if moisture is available, termites can
          be away from the soil for extended times."</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Read that against what a West Texas property actually looks like.</strong> Landscape
          irrigation running through a summer. Air conditioning condensate discharging to the same patch of
          dirt for four months. A swamp cooler line. In a region with sixteen inches of annual rainfall,
          those artificial sources are not marginal — they are frequently the only reliable moisture within
          a hundred feet, and everything that needs water knows where they are.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The closest documented analogue is Colorado</strong>, and we flag it as an analogue
          rather than as Texas evidence. Colorado State Extension is explicit that termite problems there
          have <em>increased</em> because of elevated moisture from landscape irrigation and poor drainage,
          buried wood, and inadequate termite-proofing driven by the misconception that there are no
          termites in Colorado. The belief causes the under-protection, and the under-protection causes the
          damage.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What we will not claim.</strong> We could not find any AgriLife publication that
          quantifies termite pressure for the High Plains or the Permian Basin specifically, and none that
          says this region is lower-risk than east Texas. So we are not going to give you a regional
          percentage, and anybody who does is inventing it. What we can tell you is that the dry-climate
          reasoning does not hold, and that the sources say so.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The desert termite — what you probably have in the pasture</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          This is the most useful piece of local knowledge on this page, and it is one that saves people
          money rather than costing them any.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you have land out here, you have very likely seen thin sheets and fragile tubes of dried mud
          on fence posts, on dead grass, on cow dung, on the base of mesquite. It looks alarming and it is
          routinely mistaken for a structural termite problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It is almost certainly the desert termite,</strong> <em>Gnathamitermes tubiformans</em>,
          and AgriLife's description is unambiguous. Common in south and west Texas. Conspicuous from May
          through September on shortgrass pasture. Builds fragile mud tubes and sheets of mud around the
          surfaces of herbaceous and woody plants, litter, dung, fence posts and similar objects. Workers
          about three-eighths of an inch, creamy white with brown heads.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the three facts that matter:</strong> it does not tunnel into wood, it causes no
          structural damage, and AgriLife describes it as medically harmless to man and animals.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What it actually is</strong> is a rangeland forage issue. It feeds on living and dry
          forage grasses, and it is worse on overgrazed or drought-stressed ground — which makes its
          presence a grazing-management signal rather than a pest control emergency.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>How to tell them apart in practice.</strong> Location and material. Desert termite
          workings are out in the open on vegetation, litter and dung, in pasture, and they are fragile —
          they crumble at a touch. Subterranean termite shelter tubes are on structures: piers, foundation
          walls, slab edges, near utility penetrations, running vertically toward wood, and they are
          tougher and more purposeful. Mud on your fence line is one thing. Mud on your foundation is
          another.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why a pest company tells you this.</strong> Because the alternative is quoting a termite
          job on a pasture phenomenon that was never going to hurt anything. That happens, and it should
          not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Which species are actually here</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Worth being precise about, because national content generalises badly.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Native subterranean termites,</strong> <em>Reticulitermes</em> — principally the eastern
          subterranean termite <em>R. flavipes</em> — are what we write about for this region, and what we
          treat for.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Two other Texas <em>Reticulitermes</em> species stop east of here.</strong>
          <em>R. virginicus</em> is recorded from east Texas west to Uvalde and Tom Green counties.
          <em>R. hageni</em> reaches Bexar and Eastland. Neither range extends into the Permian Basin or
          onto the Llano Estacado.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Formosan termites are not here.</strong> <em>Coptotermes formosanus</em> is recorded in
          Texas from the Houston-Galveston and Beaumont-Port Arthur areas. It is a genuinely more
          aggressive animal and it gets invoked in marketing far outside its range. If a company mentions
          Formosan termites in Gaines County, that is a flag.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And one we deliberately do not claim.</strong> The arid-land subterranean termite,
          <em>R. tibialis</em>, is well documented for Arizona and Colorado, and it would be a tidy fit for
          this region. We could not find a Texas extension source placing it in Texas, so we are not going
          to assert it. If that changes, this page changes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The colony, and what the castes tell you</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Workers</strong> are blind, wingless, white to creamy white, a quarter to three-eighths
          of an inch. They do all the feeding and all the damage.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Soldiers</strong> look like workers but with well-developed brownish heads and strong
          mandibles. They make up only one to five percent of a colony, which is why finding one is
          informative — there are a great many workers behind it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Swarmers</strong> — the reproductives — run about a quarter to three-eighths of an inch,
          coal black through to pale yellow-brown, with pale or smoky-grey wings.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Timing tells you about age.</strong> A colony takes two to four years to produce
          swarmers, and E-368 puts maturity at three to five years. A mature native colony is described as
          around two hundred and fifty thousand workers, though many are much smaller. So a swarm inside
          your house is evidence of something established years ago, not something that arrived recently —
          which is the argument for inspection rather than for waiting to see whether it recurs.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What mud tubes are made of, and why that matters.</strong> AgriLife describes shelter
          tubes as particles of soil or wood and bits of debris held together by salivary and faecal
          secretions. They serve three purposes: protection while foraging above ground, bridging masonry
          barriers the termites cannot eat through, and carrying moist soil up into the structure so the
          colony can work away from the ground. That third function is the one that connects back to the
          dryness argument — the tube is how they take their humidity with them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Termite or flying ant?</strong> Two checks and they are both easy. A termite has no
          pinched waist; an ant does. A termite's four wings are equal in length; an ant's front wings are
          longer than the rear pair. Keep a few in a bag or take a close photograph — identification off a
          swarmer is straightforward and it settles the question immediately.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>April and May — the local swarm window</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Swarm timing in Texas runs on a strong north-south gradient, and this is one of the places where
          statewide advice actively misleads.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>AgriLife:</strong> "Swarming begins in South Texas in January and February; in the
          Panhandle region of Texas, swarms do not occur until April and May."
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>So the defensible window for these counties is April and May.</strong> If you read
          statewide guidance saying February to May, that range is the whole state stacked together and the
          early end of it belongs to the Rio Grande Valley.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The triggers are heat, day length and moisture.</strong> Flights occur during daylight
          hours, and typically after rain.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What to do with that.</strong> A termite inspection in late winter or very early spring —
          February or March — sits ahead of the local swarm rather than behind it. That is also the
          quietest part of our year and the easiest time to get scheduled, which is a useful coincidence
          rather than a sales pitch.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And if you do see a swarm indoors</strong>, collect some. Swarmers inside a structure are
          the strongest single piece of evidence available, far better than a description, and they are
          gone within hours.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Slab and pier-and-beam behave differently</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>On a slab</strong>, AgriLife's account is that termites enter through wood in direct
          contact with soil, and by building shelter tubes over or through cracks in foundations. The
          specific vulnerabilities are bath traps and utility penetrations — the openings cut through the
          slab for plumbing, which are frequently oversized and rarely sealed. Removable access hatches at
          bath traps are recommended, and post-construction treatment often requires drilling.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The problem with a slab is not that it prevents termites. It is that it hides them.</strong>
          Activity can run inside a wall for a long time with nothing visible from the interior, which is
          why the exterior foundation line and the penetrations matter so much on an inspection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On pier-and-beam</strong>, AgriLife gives construction figures worth knowing: a minimum
          of eighteen inches of clearance between floor joists and soil, and at least twelve inches between
          floor beams and soil. Cover at least seventy percent of the crawl space soil with six-mil
          polyethylene. Cross-ventilate at one square foot of vent per hundred and fifty square feet of
          crawl space, with vents opposite one another and near the corners.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The advantage of a crawl space is that it can be inspected.</strong> The disadvantage is
          that it usually is not, and a crawl space with poor ventilation and no vapour barrier is a
          humidity chamber under your floor.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Conducive conditions — the free half of the job</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          AgriLife's cultural measures, which cost far less than treatment and make treatment work harder.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Grade sloping away from the structure on all sides.</strong> Water should leave the
          building, not collect against it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Soil level at least six to eight inches below the top of the foundation.</strong> Raised
          beds and added fill that bury the foundation line are one of the most common faults we find, and
          they defeat both the barrier and the inspection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Remove all wood-to-soil contact.</strong> AgriLife names fence posts, stair carriages,
          trellises and door facings. Every one of those is a bridge over whatever barrier exists.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Remove form stakes, wood scraps and stumps</strong> left from construction. Buried wood
          against a new building is a starter colony with a delivery address.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Avoid dense shrubbery against the house.</strong> AgriLife gives two reasons and both
          matter: it raises humidity at the wall, and it blocks inspection of the foundation line. A
          property you cannot inspect is a property with no early warning.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Separate porches and steps from soil</strong> by at least six inches of solid concrete —
          the junction between a slab porch and the house is a classic hidden entry, because nobody can see
          it and nothing about it looks wrong from outside.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And fix the water.</strong> Defective plumbing, leaky roofs, air conditioning condenser
          leaks, poorly maintained gutters. In this climate these are not maintenance trivia — they are
          frequently the entire reason a termite problem is possible on a property that would otherwise be
          too dry to support one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What the numbers do and do not tell you</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          AgriLife's E-368 puts termite damage in Texas at roughly five hundred million dollars a year,
          and states that an unprotected home has approximately a seventy percent chance of termite damage
          within twenty-five years.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Those are state-level figures and we are labelling them as such.</strong> Texas includes
          the Gulf coast, where pressure is at its highest and where Formosan termites occur. Applying a
          statewide percentage to a property in Gaines County as though it were a local risk figure would
          be exactly the kind of move this site exists not to make.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What the figures are genuinely useful for</strong> is the shape of the problem rather
          than its local magnitude: termite damage is slow, cumulative, and largely invisible until it is
          substantial, which is why it is measured over twenty-five years rather than in incidents.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the honest local position</strong> is the one this page opened with. We do not know
          the West Texas number, nobody has published it, the dry-climate reasoning does not hold, and the
          cost of finding out the hard way is structural. An inspection is cheap by comparison, and if it
          finds nothing at all, that is a good outcome rather than a wasted visit.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-3);font-size:.95rem">
          Biology, construction standards and management guidance on this page follow Texas A&amp;M
          AgriLife Extension publications, principally E-368, together with the Texas Insects field guide
          and AgriLife urban entomology material. Where a claim could not be sourced for this region, the
          page says so rather than filling the gap.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Termite questions" />
  <Cta
    line="If you have found mud on a fence post rather than on a foundation, say so — that is very likely a desert termite and a much shorter conversation. Swarmers indoors are the ones worth keeping in a bag."
  />
</BaseLayout>
